1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Hand-carved bars and Victorian water filters,

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:11,000
liquor guns and brew kettles, crown caps and secret wine

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:12,000
cellars.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Now, saloons on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:29,001
Billy Bob's in the Fort Worth, Texas, Historic Stockyards District.

6
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:39,700
120,000 square feet of space, nearly three acres.

7
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:44,960
It's a Texas-sized saloon that can hold up to 6,000 customers.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
This entertainment center is so massive, it has 32 bar stations.

9
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:56,240
Two of the bars are 100 feet long.

10
00:00:57,580 --> 00:00:59,200
Bottled beer is their biggest seller.

11
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:05,239
Back in 1986, 16,000 bottles sold at the Hank Williams Jr.

12
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:07,520
concert in about two hours.

13
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:13,380
It was also in the mid-'80s when Merle Haggard bought a round of Canadian Club.

14
00:01:13,381 --> 00:01:17,840
For the entire house, 5,000 people.

15
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:21,280
The singer's tab, $13,000.

16
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:26,380
And Billy Bob's made it into the Guinness Book of World Records.

17
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:34,880
All that and a half million dollars worth of video games, slot machines, pool tables, you name it.

18
00:01:36,540 --> 00:01:39,500
Billy Bob's calls itself the world's largest honky-tonk.

19
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,020
But it might be even more.

20
00:01:42,021 --> 00:01:46,120
Inside the saloon, there's a professional bull riding rink.

21
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:51,700
An arena where rodeo cowboys ride some of the toughest bulls in Texas.

22
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:56,840
The top names in country western and western rock music headline here.

23
00:01:57,940 --> 00:02:01,380
Talking about entertainment and all the major stars playing here,

24
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:03,840
I've got an old saying here at Billy Bob's,

25
00:02:03,841 --> 00:02:05,960
we get them on their way up.

26
00:02:07,220 --> 00:02:11,160
And somewhere in the middle, but if they move on to the bigger venues,

27
00:02:11,161 --> 00:02:14,860
They go away from us, but it's a funny thing.

28
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:16,400
They always seem to come back.

29
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:24,920
And so do the customers, not just to be entertained with great music, but also to have a drink.

30
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:30,040
Bottled beer is definitely the most popular beverage in Billy Bob's.

31
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:34,340
About 68% of all of our beverage sales are a long-neck bottle of beer.

32
00:02:34,341 --> 00:02:41,480
Besides serving up long-neck beers, the bartenders pour all the alcohol the old-fashioned way,

33
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:45,760
directly from bottles that are capped with modern pour spouts.

34
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:51,180
The bartender's toolkit includes shot glasses and jiggers.

35
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:56,680
But how does the management here know, at the end of the night,

36
00:02:56,940 --> 00:02:59,540
how many jiggers of whiskey or vodka were poured?

37
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:01,940
Or the number of cocktails?

38
00:03:01,941 --> 00:03:12,320
At this saloon, they use electronic scales and a hand count.

39
00:03:12,321 --> 00:03:18,120
Considering the massive size and the number of customers even on a slow night, amazingly,

40
00:03:18,121 --> 00:03:20,660
it works.

41
00:03:20,661 --> 00:03:26,180
Liquor sales only amount to about 35% of our total sales here.

42
00:03:26,181 --> 00:03:33,360
And everyone's talked about going to computerized or automatic liquor guns.

43
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:40,680
And the main reason that I've never done it is we're three acres, 100,000 square feet,

44
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:44,560
and you've got to run extensive lines for liquor guns.

45
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:49,900
And they say your cost of sales are better in automation.

46
00:03:49,901 --> 00:03:52,700
Till this day, you're not going to convince me of that.

47
00:03:52,701 --> 00:04:00,660
I've got a foolproof system, a little bit of labor intensified, but that also creates jobs for some people.

48
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:08,160
At the end of the night at every single bar station that served drinks, this hand count swings into action.

49
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:13,920
With two people, the bartender for that station and a Billy Bob's manager.

50
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:20,620
Well, from the beginning, we'd start out with an initial inventory of all of our liquor.

51
00:04:20,621 --> 00:04:23,260
we'd establish what the weights are for each bottle,

52
00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:25,980
each brand individually, and have that beginning number.

53
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:28,880
Then at the end of each evening,

54
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:31,220
the inventory process begins again.

55
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:36,980
So we know how much liquor was served weight-wise

56
00:04:36,981 --> 00:04:38,580
from each bar station, how many ounces,

57
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:40,940
and subtract that from the beginning weight,

58
00:04:41,020 --> 00:04:44,220
and then that establishes the usage in ounces

59
00:04:44,221 --> 00:04:45,500
for the alcohol for that evening.

60
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:49,140
And we're able to take those values,

61
00:04:49,141 --> 00:04:53,840
the total alcohol that was served versus the sales determine how far off the bartender was

62
00:04:53,841 --> 00:04:54,780
or if they poured perfect.

63
00:04:55,420 --> 00:05:01,800
The next step would be to count all the beer, and we carry about 17 varieties of beer, Billy Bob's,

64
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:05,360
and we have a beginning count on the beer and an ending count on the beer,

65
00:05:05,460 --> 00:05:08,180
and obviously the difference between the two is the sales for that bar station.

66
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:11,740
In addition to that, we serve draft beer.

67
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:14,040
The draft beer is controlled through a cup count.

68
00:05:14,620 --> 00:05:18,520
The bartender has a beginning count of cups and an ending count,

69
00:05:18,521 --> 00:05:22,480
And again, the difference between the two is the amount of product that is served.

70
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:28,500
Like the draft beer, the soft drinks, juices, and wine are handled with a cup count.

71
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,500
Incredibly, on a night when Billy Bob serves as many as 5,500 patrons,

72
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:38,300
this process takes just a bit more than an hour.

73
00:05:40,060 --> 00:05:43,800
Naturally, I'm a Texan, and maybe I've got old ways,

74
00:05:43,801 --> 00:05:53,320
But when you take a liquor gun and you pour liquor into a whiskey glass and then Coke on top of it, to me you lose part of the romance.

75
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:59,000
A Texan wants to see his real thing go in that shot glass and then into that liquor glass.

76
00:05:59,760 --> 00:06:02,280
And that's one of the main reasons why I do it.

77
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:10,120
And I can control my cost of sales just as easy or maybe better than an automated system can.

78
00:06:10,121 --> 00:06:12,760
Now, the person that's selling that automated system

79
00:06:12,761 --> 00:06:14,940
is not going to believe that,

80
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:16,960
but bring him here and I'll prove him wrong.

81
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:19,980
Jay Miller wouldn't agree.

82
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:23,560
He's the general manager for Yankee Doodle's sports bar

83
00:06:23,561 --> 00:06:25,000
in Woodland Hills, California.

84
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:28,040
We call it our liquor gun.

85
00:06:28,580 --> 00:06:31,480
Each gun has up to 16 brands of alcohol,

86
00:06:32,440 --> 00:06:34,880
and it's pretty impressive.

87
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:39,720
It's called an easy bar.

88
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:44,260
It looks something like your TV's remote control, but with a hose attached.

89
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:50,760
His liquor dispensing system is equipped with a microprocessor that's been hooked up to the bar's PC.

90
00:06:52,240 --> 00:06:55,080
All the bartender has to do is press the right button.

91
00:06:56,580 --> 00:07:01,360
In this way, the saloon owner can choose the pour size for every drink the bartender makes

92
00:07:01,361 --> 00:07:04,700
by simply pre-programming the numbers into his computer.

93
00:07:04,701 --> 00:07:14,660
The Easy Bar software called Barfly can also report each liquor gun's activity and track

94
00:07:14,661 --> 00:07:20,080
the alcohol by brand, price and bar station.

95
00:07:20,081 --> 00:07:26,360
With this system, pouring a drink doesn't begin at the bar.

96
00:07:26,361 --> 00:07:31,380
Where the liquor starts from is in our liquor room, which is way in the back of the building,

97
00:07:31,381 --> 00:07:35,380
that right out of the bottle we pour it into what we call the tree.

98
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:39,020
And from the tree, it's pumped all the way out to the well

99
00:07:39,021 --> 00:07:40,820
where you're ordering your drink.

100
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:45,760
And when you press that button, a shot of that same alcohol,

101
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:50,440
same brand from the way in the back is actually coming to your glass in front of you.

102
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:55,600
Draft Beer works on a similar principle with a few extras.

103
00:07:55,601 --> 00:08:05,600
We have an interesting unit that pumps out nitrogen and CO2 to push the beer through

104
00:08:05,601 --> 00:08:08,600
the lines.

105
00:08:08,601 --> 00:08:16,100
And it helps to make it more consistent and have the right foam and head for each beer

106
00:08:16,101 --> 00:08:19,680
and any one of our bartenders can pour it at any time and it comes out the same way

107
00:08:19,681 --> 00:08:24,680
each and every time.

108
00:08:24,681 --> 00:08:31,060
The second part of our beer system is what keeps the beer cold.

109
00:08:31,061 --> 00:08:36,360
We use two separate units to chill it and to keep it cold all the way up to the tap,

110
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:37,900
right where it comes out of the spigot.

111
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:44,500
As far as actually tapping up a keg, it's just a matter of connecting it and locking it down.

112
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:48,540
And once that's done, the system does the rest.

113
00:08:48,541 --> 00:08:55,380
When it comes to the corks or the caps on their liquor bottles,

114
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:58,380
most saloons replace them with a standard pour spout.

115
00:08:59,560 --> 00:09:02,080
But some innovative pour spouts have hit the market

116
00:09:02,081 --> 00:09:04,800
with a computer chip right inside the pour spout.

117
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:07,740
It has a little light on the side.

118
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:11,780
And when the bartender picks up the bottle and pours the drink,

119
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:15,160
the light will blink, and each blink is a half ounce.

120
00:09:15,161 --> 00:09:21,500
We have a small computer that's handheld with a ring attached,

121
00:09:21,820 --> 00:09:27,160
which can be inserted over the top quickly of every liquor bottle spout

122
00:09:27,161 --> 00:09:32,180
and give you a complete liquor report as to what each bottle, each brand,

123
00:09:32,420 --> 00:09:35,200
how much was poured, and combine it all together.

124
00:09:36,320 --> 00:09:40,120
All this mechanization might be fine in the 21st century,

125
00:09:41,120 --> 00:09:44,520
but saloon life in the Old West didn't begin this way.

126
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:58,660
Next, at the end of a grueling day, a weary miner was grateful just to get a drink.

127
00:09:58,661 --> 00:10:06,040
Americans consumed nearly 6 billion gallons of beer in 1992, enough to fill the Houston

128
00:10:06,041 --> 00:10:13,120
Astrodome over 12 times.

129
00:10:13,121 --> 00:10:18,400
It was the 49ers, the mountain men, and the trail-driving cowboys who blazed a pathway

130
00:10:18,401 --> 00:10:22,920
west for what became the truly American version of a saloon.

131
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:32,160
The word saloon comes from the French word, salon, and conjures up an image of a rather

132
00:10:32,161 --> 00:10:40,580
elegant place to pass the time, in good conversation, with educated people, sipping civilized alcoholic

133
00:10:40,581 --> 00:10:41,356
beverages.

134
00:10:41,357 --> 00:10:47,640
The American East Coast had its share of these civilized drinking establishments.

135
00:10:49,260 --> 00:10:52,667
The Western Saloon was another story.

136
00:10:52,668 --> 00:10:57,180
The very earliest saloons in the West were really

137
00:10:57,181 --> 00:11:01,690
not at all like what their Eastern counterparts appeared to be.

138
00:11:01,691 --> 00:11:03,480
In the West, quite often,

139
00:11:04,040 --> 00:11:07,391
they were an offshoot of the Suttler's Tents from the Civil War.

140
00:11:07,392 --> 00:11:08,700
At the front of the tent,

141
00:11:08,701 --> 00:11:09,800
They would open up the flaps.

142
00:11:10,140 --> 00:11:13,200
They'd have a couple of beer barrels sitting there with planks across them,

143
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:14,700
and that served as the bar.

144
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:22,240
Inside was a little bit of comfort, a few friendly faces, and a stiff drink,

145
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:26,840
a place where a man could throw down a tin cup full of freshly distilled whiskey

146
00:11:26,841 --> 00:11:29,840
or warm beer ladled out of a wooden barrel.

147
00:11:32,300 --> 00:11:38,220
As the towns grew, the old tent saloons gave way to more modern one-story wooden cabins.

148
00:11:38,700 --> 00:11:45,700
In the best of all worlds, the saloon keeper managed to buy a piano and find someone to

149
00:11:46,540 --> 00:11:47,240
play it.

150
00:11:47,241 --> 00:11:53,540
In hotter parts of the Old West, like Tombstone, Arizona, a set of batwing doors

151
00:11:56,620 --> 00:12:02,460
offered a cool breeze and hid just enough of the shenanigans going on inside from the

152
00:12:02,461 --> 00:12:04,780
ladies and children who might pass by.

153
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:14,800
Ultimately, a western saloon was only as good as its bar, something solid to lean on with

154
00:12:14,801 --> 00:12:17,300
a brass rail where a man could hook his boot heel.

155
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:21,620
The brass foot rail in a bar actually serves a very important purpose.

156
00:12:21,621 --> 00:12:26,280
If you're standing at a bar drinking over a long period of time, your back will actually

157
00:12:26,281 --> 00:12:27,540
start aching, your lower back.

158
00:12:27,541 --> 00:12:32,380
But if you raise one foot up on this rail just a little bit, it'll straighten out your spine.

159
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:36,380
It'll make it a lot easier to stand there for quite a while drinking.

160
00:12:36,960 --> 00:12:43,060
By the mid-19th century, highly skilled German woodcarvers arrived as part of the wave of German immigration.

161
00:12:44,780 --> 00:12:48,620
Companies like the Brunswick-Balke-Colander Company of Chicago hired them.

162
00:12:49,820 --> 00:12:54,780
In the production of elaborate billiard tables and bars that were carved by hand

163
00:12:54,781 --> 00:12:59,160
and are still being carved today, like these by John Walker.

164
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:05,740
The blocks of wood in the mid-19th century would have been shaped in the ordinary way by a carpenter.

165
00:13:05,860 --> 00:13:08,780
Of course, any good woodcarver is also a carpenter,

166
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:12,220
so he would have shaped his blocks of wood using whatever tools were available.

167
00:13:12,221 --> 00:13:16,960
He would have used knives something like these, although these are modern versions.

168
00:13:17,580 --> 00:13:24,380
They're based on old shapes, which were worked out carefully by carvers who made their own knives,

169
00:13:24,381 --> 00:13:31,640
Once carved, these bars could be shipped anywhere in the world, even around Cape Horn,

170
00:13:32,460 --> 00:13:35,260
carried overland by train or delivered by wagon.

171
00:13:37,760 --> 00:13:41,780
There was also the back bar, with its mirror and display shelves.

172
00:13:42,700 --> 00:13:46,240
It was a separate piece of furniture that was placed back from the bar,

173
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:52,060
flush against a wall, giving the bartender about three feet of room in which to serve his customers.

174
00:13:54,380 --> 00:13:58,760
Legend has it that the back bar mirror was created so that a man could lean on the bar,

175
00:13:58,960 --> 00:14:02,240
drink his whiskey, and watch his back at the same time.

176
00:14:03,700 --> 00:14:06,840
In fact, the reason for the mirror was much less romantic.

177
00:14:09,620 --> 00:14:13,340
One of the prized possessions was the large back mirror,

178
00:14:13,860 --> 00:14:17,320
the mirror that indicated that this was a dignified place.

179
00:14:17,321 --> 00:14:21,620
It also had the effect of doubling the apparent size of the room

180
00:14:21,621 --> 00:14:24,440
because it would reflect the whole thing into the mirror

181
00:14:24,441 --> 00:14:27,540
and not give you the feel that you were in such a narrow place,

182
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:28,940
which is in fact where you were.

183
00:14:30,220 --> 00:14:31,980
And the drinks these men expected?

184
00:14:32,860 --> 00:14:34,180
Straight whiskey or beer.

185
00:14:35,240 --> 00:14:38,640
The whiskey was sometimes distilled by the saloon keeper himself,

186
00:14:39,340 --> 00:14:43,720
but more often it arrived by wagon from distilleries in the east and south.

187
00:14:47,740 --> 00:14:50,600
But beer was volatile and easily spoiled.

188
00:14:51,620 --> 00:14:54,440
Unlike whiskey, it could not stay fresh on a trip west.

189
00:14:55,580 --> 00:14:58,560
As a result, in the early days of the Western Saloon,

190
00:14:59,280 --> 00:15:02,940
beer was brewed close to the saloon, sometimes on site.

191
00:15:04,860 --> 00:15:07,860
Once it was brewed, the beer was poured into wooden barrels.

192
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:12,380
These beer barrels were constructed from wooden stays

193
00:15:12,381 --> 00:15:15,100
that were held in place by four iron hoops.

194
00:15:16,320 --> 00:15:18,260
At the top was a bunghole,

195
00:15:18,720 --> 00:15:20,700
where the beer was poured in at the brewery

196
00:15:20,701 --> 00:15:22,000
and tapped at the saloon.

197
00:15:23,560 --> 00:15:26,240
Lower down on the front of the barrel was a draft hole.

198
00:15:27,640 --> 00:15:29,840
Each opening was sealed with a wooden peg

199
00:15:29,841 --> 00:15:32,000
that was literally whacked into place

200
00:15:32,001 --> 00:15:34,020
by a man using a bung hammer.

201
00:15:35,300 --> 00:15:37,080
This was a good-sized mallet,

202
00:15:37,520 --> 00:15:39,260
fashioned from wood and a bit of metal.

203
00:15:40,400 --> 00:15:42,440
The ends needed to be soft to prevent the hammer

204
00:15:42,441 --> 00:15:43,480
from cracking the barrel.

205
00:15:46,420 --> 00:15:48,800
The barrels also were lined with pitch

206
00:15:48,801 --> 00:15:50,060
in order to seal them.

207
00:15:50,700 --> 00:15:52,680
This isn't like a tar or anything like that.

208
00:15:52,700 --> 00:15:57,620
It was a flavored pitch that would seal the barrel,

209
00:15:57,720 --> 00:15:59,680
would keep the wood from absorbing the beer,

210
00:15:59,880 --> 00:16:02,300
and would also impart certain taste to the beer.

211
00:16:02,640 --> 00:16:05,040
That's why the recipe for the pitch at a brewery

212
00:16:05,041 --> 00:16:07,400
was as secret as the recipe for their beers was,

213
00:16:07,520 --> 00:16:10,500
because it did have an influence on how the beer tasted.

214
00:16:13,420 --> 00:16:15,900
When the saloon owner was ready to open the keg,

215
00:16:16,360 --> 00:16:18,480
he stood it on end in order to top it.

216
00:16:18,481 --> 00:16:22,480
A simple wooden spigot was inserted into the bunghole.

217
00:16:22,481 --> 00:16:26,480
Next, he lifted his keg onto a wooden cradle,

218
00:16:26,481 --> 00:16:30,480
called a stillage, and laid the keg on its side.

219
00:16:30,481 --> 00:16:34,480
Finally, he knocked out the peg covering the draft hole.

220
00:16:34,481 --> 00:16:38,480
This would let some air in, allowing the beer to flow easily when it was poured.

221
00:16:38,481 --> 00:16:42,480
If the saloon keeper was a bit fastidious,

222
00:16:42,481 --> 00:16:46,480
he might cover the draft hole with a cloth to keep the dirt out.

223
00:16:46,481 --> 00:16:51,480
Once opened, the beer would go flat and it had to be served within a few days,

224
00:16:51,481 --> 00:16:55,480
because contact with the air would spoil it.

225
00:16:57,480 --> 00:17:03,480
If a customer wanted to buy some beer to take with him, he brought his own growler to the barkeep.

226
00:17:03,481 --> 00:17:06,480
They were workers' lunch pails, basically.

227
00:17:06,481 --> 00:17:11,480
They would be a metal pail with an enamel finish on it, and the worker would have a lid on it.

228
00:17:11,481 --> 00:17:14,480
The worker would pack their lunch in that during the day,

229
00:17:14,481 --> 00:17:18,780
and when it came to lunchtime, they would get their lunch out

230
00:17:18,781 --> 00:17:22,300
and then he'd give all these growlers to usually what they call the bucket boy,

231
00:17:22,420 --> 00:17:28,180
which is either a retired worker or, in some cases, a newly hired younger person.

232
00:17:28,560 --> 00:17:32,320
And that bucket boy would go to the neighborhood saloon and get him filled with beer

233
00:17:32,321 --> 00:17:34,940
and then bring him back for them to drink with their lunch at lunchtime.

234
00:17:37,960 --> 00:17:43,740
The Western Saloon was also a place where thirsty citizens could find a substitute for fresh water.

235
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:47,100
In most cases, the only place.

236
00:17:48,240 --> 00:17:52,140
Unlike the East, natural drinking water was often scarce in the Old West.

237
00:17:53,980 --> 00:17:58,680
Mining towns also suffered from water tainted by bacteria and alkaline deposits.

238
00:17:59,780 --> 00:18:03,980
The only bottled water available was expensive imported mineral water

239
00:18:03,981 --> 00:18:07,120
or artificially carbonated soda water

240
00:18:07,121 --> 00:18:11,920
manufactured by soda works in towns like Sacramento and San Francisco.

241
00:18:11,921 --> 00:18:22,220
Even if clean water wasn't flowing, by the 1870s, money was.

242
00:18:22,960 --> 00:18:26,200
Next, with financing from the Midwestern breweries,

243
00:18:26,640 --> 00:18:30,760
the humble Western Saloon was about to become a drinking palace.

244
00:18:33,140 --> 00:18:36,180
The word whiskey evolved from the Celtic word,

245
00:18:36,660 --> 00:18:40,260
to the English it sounded like whisky,

246
00:18:40,261 --> 00:18:43,700
and so it was finally corrupted to whiskey.

247
00:18:46,340 --> 00:18:47,360
Virginia City.

248
00:18:48,200 --> 00:18:49,480
In the 21st century,

249
00:18:50,280 --> 00:18:53,160
a lingering memory of the heyday of the Western Saloon.

250
00:18:54,080 --> 00:18:56,920
Bucket of Blood, the old Washoe Club.

251
00:18:57,620 --> 00:18:59,520
Rich or poor, these were the only places

252
00:18:59,521 --> 00:19:02,780
a man could buy a glass of beer or a shot of whiskey.

253
00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:08,700
The White Elephant Saloon down in Fort Worth, Texas

254
00:19:08,701 --> 00:19:10,820
also conjures up the Old West.

255
00:19:12,780 --> 00:19:15,580
It was here 120 years ago

256
00:19:15,581 --> 00:19:19,320
that Bat Masterson and Butch Cassidy enjoyed the hospitality.

257
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:26,220
In the 19th century, for an independent saloon keeper

258
00:19:26,221 --> 00:19:29,060
who wanted to set up a drinking establishment like this one,

259
00:19:29,620 --> 00:19:33,740
on his own, the bar, the back bar, tables, chairs,

260
00:19:34,140 --> 00:19:35,720
glassware, or a billiard table,

261
00:19:36,120 --> 00:19:38,300
it represented an enormous investment.

262
00:19:38,700 --> 00:19:46,900
By the late 1870s, the large breweries were in fierce competition for markets.

263
00:19:49,540 --> 00:19:51,340
The next step was obvious.

264
00:19:52,360 --> 00:19:53,700
Buy into the saloon business.

265
00:19:54,660 --> 00:19:58,920
In this way, the brewery could ensure a steady stream of customers who would buy their product.

266
00:20:02,120 --> 00:20:04,140
They hired brewery agents.

267
00:20:04,141 --> 00:20:09,540
salesmen who came to town offering to supply the necessary fixtures on credit,

268
00:20:10,960 --> 00:20:12,740
sometimes at low interest rates.

269
00:20:14,740 --> 00:20:19,180
The brewery required only that the saloon keeper agree to sell the brewer's beer,

270
00:20:19,640 --> 00:20:23,160
and only his beer, while the debt was being repaid.

271
00:20:25,100 --> 00:20:27,660
These arrangements could last for years,

272
00:20:28,540 --> 00:20:33,360
in the process securing for the brewer a long-term dependable source of revenue.

273
00:20:34,140 --> 00:20:41,520
In other cases, the brewery simply bought the saloon outright and hired a manager to

274
00:20:41,521 --> 00:20:44,860
run it.

275
00:20:44,861 --> 00:20:52,540
It was in this way that the once ordinary saloon was reinvented.

276
00:20:52,541 --> 00:20:59,720
Simple gas lamps were replaced by crystal chandeliers.

277
00:20:59,721 --> 00:21:05,380
black bars became the centerpiece of the saloon, with polished mahogany shelves displaying

278
00:21:05,381 --> 00:21:11,980
imported liquors and expensive crystal glassware.

279
00:21:11,981 --> 00:21:15,060
These mirrors actually were the neon signs of their day.

280
00:21:15,061 --> 00:21:21,100
What you get in the 19th century that really does affect the Western saloon is this growth

281
00:21:21,101 --> 00:21:23,920
in interest and leisure time.

282
00:21:23,921 --> 00:21:28,340
The idea that people should actually have some time set aside where they should enjoy

283
00:21:28,341 --> 00:21:31,540
themselves is really a 19th century phenomenon.

284
00:21:31,760 --> 00:21:33,680
It starts becoming more and more popular.

285
00:21:35,640 --> 00:21:40,380
The Transcontinental Railroad made every bit of this extravagance possible.

286
00:21:43,480 --> 00:21:48,260
To begin with, whiskey and other distilled spirits could be shipped with greater speed

287
00:21:48,261 --> 00:21:49,940
from the distillery to the saloon.

288
00:21:53,540 --> 00:21:57,360
Beer, which had become the more popular drink, was another story.

289
00:21:58,340 --> 00:22:05,860
In the last three decades of the 19th century, a burst of invention began to transform the

290
00:22:05,861 --> 00:22:07,862
saloon business.

291
00:22:07,863 --> 00:22:16,620
Revolutionary bottle capping devices, artificial refrigeration, modern

292
00:22:16,621 --> 00:22:24,900
beer tapping mechanisms. This part of the story begins with the French scientist Dr.

293
00:22:24,901 --> 00:22:30,140
Louis Pasteur and the discovery of what came to be called pasteurization.

294
00:22:31,520 --> 00:22:33,960
Keeping beer cool has always been important.

295
00:22:34,340 --> 00:22:35,700
Beer is a foodstuff.

296
00:22:35,780 --> 00:22:40,720
It's like a bread or any other type of packaged food that's already prepared, and it will

297
00:22:40,721 --> 00:22:41,421
go bad.

298
00:22:42,040 --> 00:22:43,273
By the 1860s, Dr.

299
00:22:43,274 --> 00:22:48,640
Pasteur discovered that beer often contained microorganisms that literally

300
00:22:48,641 --> 00:22:50,800
made some people who drank it sick.

301
00:22:51,700 --> 00:22:55,680
By heating it at 140 degrees Fahrenheit for about 20 minutes,

302
00:22:56,380 --> 00:22:59,780
the bacteria would be killed and spoilage would not occur.

303
00:23:00,700 --> 00:23:05,460
This meant that beer could be shipped to saloons over longer distances without turning sour.

304
00:23:05,980 --> 00:23:12,420
With one caveat, it had to be stored at about 70 degrees Fahrenheit to prohibit any new bacteria growth.

305
00:23:13,280 --> 00:23:16,400
This is where innovative refrigeration techniques came in.

306
00:23:20,800 --> 00:23:25,340
By the mid-1860s, natural ice supply was already an industry unto itself,

307
00:23:26,080 --> 00:23:28,180
and a large one at that.

308
00:23:32,480 --> 00:23:36,000
The ice was harvested in the northern regions like New England.

309
00:23:37,920 --> 00:23:41,320
They'd pack it in the hold of ships, put sawdust over it to insulate it,

310
00:23:41,540 --> 00:23:46,180
and actually ship it to some of these other towns and cities that were in the south.

311
00:23:46,980 --> 00:23:51,180
The ice was actually delivered by wagon from where the ship landing would be.

312
00:23:51,480 --> 00:23:53,500
These wagons would be insulated, they'd come in,

313
00:23:53,501 --> 00:23:58,380
they'd have somebody with these giant metal claws picking up the ice,

314
00:23:58,920 --> 00:24:00,600
taking it into the tavern.

315
00:24:03,400 --> 00:24:06,680
Adolphus Busch, president of the Anheuser-Busch Brewery,

316
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:09,280
took these ice wagons one step further.

317
00:24:10,740 --> 00:24:14,200
Since his beer was being shipped on the new Transcontinental Railroad,

318
00:24:14,800 --> 00:24:17,780
Bush created a network of railside ice houses.

319
00:24:19,300 --> 00:24:22,080
So that he could ship his beer, the unpasteurized beer,

320
00:24:22,120 --> 00:24:25,300
as well as the bottled pasteurized beer, in his rail cars,

321
00:24:25,680 --> 00:24:27,480
pack them, repack them with ice where they needed,

322
00:24:27,860 --> 00:24:30,320
and then continue to send them on as far as to the west coast

323
00:24:30,321 --> 00:24:32,260
or down into Arizona or Texas.

324
00:24:34,300 --> 00:24:36,720
Next came the refrigerated railroad car.

325
00:24:39,520 --> 00:24:44,160
It was patented by J.B. Sutherland of Detroit, Michigan, in 1867.

326
00:24:44,200 --> 00:24:50,460
These cars were lined with hay for insulation.

327
00:24:51,740 --> 00:24:56,420
Air would be drawn in through the bottom of the car and past the ice bunkers located at each end.

328
00:24:57,480 --> 00:25:01,180
As the cold air circulated, warm air rose to the top of the car.

329
00:25:01,860 --> 00:25:05,140
Its higher pressure forced the colder air towards the floorboards,

330
00:25:05,141 --> 00:25:08,420
where it could once again circulate past the ice bunkers.

331
00:25:08,421 --> 00:25:14,980
with this invention solidly in place by the early 1880s

332
00:25:14,981 --> 00:25:19,040
Adolphus Busch launched the beer industry's first whole fleet of

333
00:25:19,041 --> 00:25:23,800
refrigerated freight cars and the western saloon owner was

334
00:25:23,801 --> 00:25:28,000
guaranteed that he could offer his patrons a steady supply of the finest

335
00:25:28,001 --> 00:25:29,040
beer in America

336
00:25:29,041 --> 00:25:37,460
during this wave of invention beer tapping was also modernized

337
00:25:38,460 --> 00:25:41,860
The Golden Gate Safety Faucet Company of New York City

338
00:25:41,861 --> 00:25:45,320
offered its Golden Gate tapper system to saloon keepers.

339
00:25:47,260 --> 00:25:51,740
You simply pushed the faucet, the male and female sections together,

340
00:25:51,900 --> 00:25:53,340
and turned it, and that locked it,

341
00:25:53,620 --> 00:25:57,620
and then you would attach the pressure hose to the proper spigots on the backside,

342
00:25:58,160 --> 00:25:59,540
and then your system would be done.

343
00:26:01,360 --> 00:26:03,340
Because this system was airtight,

344
00:26:03,341 --> 00:26:07,120
it preserved the freshness, the flavor, and the carbonation of the beer.

345
00:26:07,460 --> 00:26:14,980
By the turn of the century, a refrigerated draft beer installation was used in combination with this tapping system.

346
00:26:18,240 --> 00:26:23,640
The pressure hoses carried carbonic gas from a pair of tanks into the top of the beer barrel.

347
00:26:24,380 --> 00:26:29,340
The gas forced the beer up through a pipe and into what was called the coil box,

348
00:26:30,480 --> 00:26:35,280
surrounded by ice chips, where it was then dispensed from a faucet at the bar.

349
00:26:37,460 --> 00:26:41,660
The keg could be under the bar itself or in the cellar.

350
00:26:44,360 --> 00:26:50,160
Also by the turn of the century, the way bottles were manufactured in court set in motion another change.

351
00:26:51,260 --> 00:26:56,400
As late as the 1880s, bottles were still made by hand in bottle shops.

352
00:26:58,020 --> 00:27:07,340
In many cases, the lip, that is the topmost part of the bottle, was added after the body of the bottle was made in this mold.

353
00:27:07,460 --> 00:27:12,760
and it was done so by adding a piece of molten glass to the top of the bottle.

354
00:27:13,900 --> 00:27:17,280
And that tended to look very crude.

355
00:27:18,060 --> 00:27:20,640
It was known as a hand-applied lip.

356
00:27:20,880 --> 00:27:22,540
One of them is called a blob top,

357
00:27:22,541 --> 00:27:27,320
and it literally looks like a piece of molten glass was smeared on top of a bottle.

358
00:27:28,600 --> 00:27:34,560
These uneven, one-of-a-kind blob tops meant that capping bottled beer had to be done by hand.

359
00:27:34,561 --> 00:27:43,260
By 1892, William Painter, the founder of the Crown, Cork and Seal Company of England,

360
00:27:43,840 --> 00:27:47,760
had patented his invention, the crown cap.

361
00:27:50,640 --> 00:27:55,840
Painter called his invention a crown because to him it resembled the crown worn by kings

362
00:27:55,841 --> 00:27:56,541
and queens.

363
00:27:58,400 --> 00:28:03,300
It was a simple object made from plain metal and lined with cork.

364
00:28:04,560 --> 00:28:07,520
The cap was positioned over the top of the bottle

365
00:28:07,521 --> 00:28:11,000
and then snapped in place by a handheld capping device.

366
00:28:13,160 --> 00:28:15,380
The basic principle of the crown sealing method

367
00:28:15,381 --> 00:28:18,600
remained unchanged for over 100 years,

368
00:28:19,880 --> 00:28:22,960
even when hand capping was replaced by an assembly line.

369
00:28:27,180 --> 00:28:28,800
Ironically, the beverage industry

370
00:28:28,801 --> 00:28:31,440
would have to wait 10 years for the bottle industry

371
00:28:31,441 --> 00:28:34,120
to perfect a thinner and more uniform lip.

372
00:28:34,560 --> 00:28:37,420
one that could accommodate painters' creation.

373
00:28:40,100 --> 00:28:43,520
Once that was accomplished, capping bottles could be mechanized,

374
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:46,220
and bottled beer could be mass-produced.

375
00:28:47,980 --> 00:28:50,860
In the period before the bottle cap became practical,

376
00:28:51,540 --> 00:28:54,500
nearly all beer was consumed in the saloon.

377
00:28:54,960 --> 00:28:59,020
But after the bottle cap, we started seeing a change in the way Americans are drinking.

378
00:28:59,021 --> 00:29:03,940
They're taking it with them on the road, taking it home, taking it outdoors.

379
00:29:04,560 --> 00:29:10,220
But the enormous impact of mass-produced bottled beer on the Western Saloon would have to wait.

380
00:29:12,760 --> 00:29:17,680
Looming on the horizon was a grassroots political movement that would sweep the nation,

381
00:29:18,360 --> 00:29:21,800
bringing the entire American liquor business to a grinding halt.

382
00:29:24,620 --> 00:29:33,940
Next, the Anti-Saloon League, well-meaning citizens who believed the saloon's overwhelming success was a national disgrace.

383
00:29:34,560 --> 00:29:48,560
When Teddy Roosevelt led a safari from East Africa to Khartoum in 1909, he brought with him one of his sons, 250 porters and guides, and 500 gallons of beer.

384
00:29:52,320 --> 00:29:56,680
She was six feet tall and weighed in at 180 pounds.

385
00:29:57,940 --> 00:30:00,420
Her husband died from acute alcoholism.

386
00:30:00,421 --> 00:30:04,580
Carrie Nation was a woman on the warpath.

387
00:30:05,640 --> 00:30:10,400
On June 1, 1900, Carrie Nation of Garrard, Kentucky,

388
00:30:10,820 --> 00:30:13,700
picked up an axe, stormed into a Kiowa, Kansas,

389
00:30:13,860 --> 00:30:16,480
drinking establishment, and made history.

390
00:30:19,840 --> 00:30:22,620
The hacked-up saloons that littered her wake

391
00:30:22,621 --> 00:30:24,080
drew even more customers,

392
00:30:25,120 --> 00:30:27,160
drinking men who were utterly spellbound

393
00:30:27,161 --> 00:30:29,040
by tales of her bold destruction.

394
00:30:30,420 --> 00:30:37,880
She belonged to the Women's Christian Temperance Union, founded in 1874.

395
00:30:39,960 --> 00:30:46,060
Yet even with all her fury, it took another organization, the Anti-Saloon League,

396
00:30:46,380 --> 00:30:53,320
and America's entry into World War I to put national alcohol prohibition over the top.

397
00:30:53,321 --> 00:31:04,300
The Anti-Saloon League arrived on the scene in the mid-1890s, a relative latecomer.

398
00:31:05,820 --> 00:31:09,800
They touted their cause with the high dollar cost of drunkenness,

399
00:31:10,800 --> 00:31:14,140
added the dark criminal elements that were part of saloon life,

400
00:31:14,520 --> 00:31:19,300
and married those to a growing pro-war, anti-German sentiment.

401
00:31:19,301 --> 00:31:28,380
it. Drinking beer, they said, supplied by Germans meant support for the Kaiser.

402
00:31:28,381 --> 00:31:29,300
In 1919,

403
00:31:29,301 --> 00:31:30,535
Congress acted.

404
00:31:30,536 --> 00:31:36,300
It passed the 18th Amendment to the Constitution of the United States.

405
00:31:36,301 --> 00:31:39,300
The 18th Amendment was an anti-business measure.

406
00:31:39,301 --> 00:31:42,300
It prohibited the manufacture, the distribution,

407
00:31:42,301 --> 00:31:44,623
and the sale of alcoholic beverages.

408
00:31:44,624 --> 00:31:48,300
It did not prohibit the possession of alcoholic beverages

409
00:31:48,301 --> 00:31:50,120
or the consumption of alcoholic beverages.

410
00:31:51,540 --> 00:31:54,920
But Congress, overriding a veto by President Wilson,

411
00:31:55,300 --> 00:31:56,540
passed the Volstead Act.

412
00:31:57,180 --> 00:31:59,240
This act defined liquor as any beverage

413
00:31:59,241 --> 00:32:01,640
containing at least 0.5% alcohol.

414
00:32:03,060 --> 00:32:05,340
Furthermore, it empowered federal agents

415
00:32:05,341 --> 00:32:06,880
to enforce the 18th Amendment

416
00:32:06,881 --> 00:32:09,660
and officially made America dry.

417
00:32:10,600 --> 00:32:14,300
It seemed prohibition was an idea that's time had come.

418
00:32:14,301 --> 00:32:19,900
The legendary saloon rode into the sunset.

419
00:32:23,180 --> 00:32:30,120
We have the feeling that the liquor traffic is so personally, morally, socially, economically,

420
00:32:30,500 --> 00:32:36,880
industrially, viciously, irredeemably antisocial, that the American people will never give it

421
00:32:36,881 --> 00:32:40,300
a permanent seat at the table of the common good.

422
00:32:40,301 --> 00:32:47,300
Meanwhile, this stunning change in the United States Constitution opened up a brand new frontier, in the East.

423
00:32:50,300 --> 00:32:57,300
The more civilized drinking establishments of cities like New York gave way to an East Coast version of a Wild West saloon.

424
00:33:00,300 --> 00:33:07,300
They called them speakeasies, because a customer had to speak easy or softly to get inside.

425
00:33:10,300 --> 00:33:13,800
The speed of life changed.

426
00:33:14,540 --> 00:33:16,060
Old bars went out of business.

427
00:33:17,620 --> 00:33:21,320
Bootleggers replaced the beer barons and legal distilleries.

428
00:33:26,200 --> 00:33:29,360
The truck replaced the refrigerated railroad car

429
00:33:29,361 --> 00:33:31,840
as the alcohol delivery vehicle of choice.

430
00:33:35,020 --> 00:33:37,940
Well, the impact of prohibition on the breweries

431
00:33:37,941 --> 00:33:39,820
was a little short of catastrophic.

432
00:33:39,821 --> 00:33:44,620
I mean, here you had a product that you were selling for many, many years that suddenly

433
00:33:44,621 --> 00:33:49,260
was declared illegal by the government, and you could no longer make and sell that product.

434
00:33:49,960 --> 00:33:53,380
So a lot of the breweries simply closed on the night of Prohibition.

435
00:33:55,260 --> 00:34:03,820
In 1929, illegal liquor was the second biggest business in Detroit at $215 million a year,

436
00:34:04,480 --> 00:34:06,180
just behind the automobile.

437
00:34:06,181 --> 00:34:13,680
The cost of alcoholic beverages skyrocketed into prohibition.

438
00:34:13,681 --> 00:34:18,780
There was one study done that showed toward the end of the 1920s that it cost 80 cents

439
00:34:18,781 --> 00:34:20,100
to buy a bottle of beer.

440
00:34:20,101 --> 00:34:22,700
Well, in today's money, that would be about $8.

441
00:34:22,701 --> 00:34:25,880
Can you imagine spending $8 for a bottle of beer?

442
00:34:25,881 --> 00:34:29,300
Or a bottle of gin would cost between $5 and $6.

443
00:34:29,301 --> 00:34:34,460
Well, in today's dollars, that's between $50 and $60.

444
00:34:34,461 --> 00:34:43,460
Along with their overpriced imported liquors, speakeasies rocketed into the mainstream.

445
00:34:43,461 --> 00:34:48,760
So did the production of freshly distilled, stomach-wrenching liquors that were reminiscent

446
00:34:48,761 --> 00:34:53,300
of the earliest days of the Western Saloon.

447
00:34:53,301 --> 00:34:55,340
Bathtub Gin may have been the most famous.

448
00:34:55,341 --> 00:34:59,340
It got its name because it was made at home, in the bathtub.

449
00:34:59,341 --> 00:35:04,660
You'd bring in a bunch of ethanol, pour it in the tub, add spices, most often juniper.

450
00:35:05,460 --> 00:35:07,220
That's what gives gin its flavor.

451
00:35:07,760 --> 00:35:09,540
And that's basically it.

452
00:35:09,580 --> 00:35:12,540
Then ladle it out of the tub, pour it in the bottles, and there you go.

453
00:35:12,600 --> 00:35:13,340
You've got gin.

454
00:35:13,700 --> 00:35:16,020
Gin is mostly cut ethanol.

455
00:35:16,580 --> 00:35:18,720
So it was very simple and easy to make.

456
00:35:19,320 --> 00:35:22,380
Probably tasted horrible and rough and fiery.

457
00:35:22,381 --> 00:35:30,820
So anything and everything was used to disguise the taste of the reconverted industrial alcohol that was being served.

458
00:35:34,960 --> 00:35:39,960
Flavored syrups that had been the preserve of the soda fountain made their debut in bars.

459
00:35:41,360 --> 00:35:46,280
At first, the bartenders relied on these syrups to sweeten the taste of the crude whiskey and gin.

460
00:35:46,281 --> 00:35:56,020
But they were soon inventing cocktails, choosing sweet vermouths instead of the syrups, creating

461
00:35:56,021 --> 00:36:00,480
drinks that would become famous, like the Old Fashioned and the Manhattan.

462
00:36:03,660 --> 00:36:09,420
In New York City, a speakeasy known as 21 secretly opened its doors for business.

463
00:36:09,421 --> 00:36:16,760
Today it is one of the most successful restaurants in New York City

464
00:36:16,761 --> 00:36:23,340
But it got its start in the lawless days of national prohibition

465
00:36:23,341 --> 00:36:29,380
On a warm summer evening in June of 1932

466
00:36:29,381 --> 00:36:32,860
Ten federal agents knocked on the door of 21

467
00:36:32,861 --> 00:36:37,400
Jimmy, the club's doorman, activated a secret alarm

468
00:36:37,401 --> 00:36:38,980
That alerted club management

469
00:36:38,981 --> 00:36:48,860
Hours of searching the premises yielded nothing.

470
00:36:50,140 --> 00:36:52,860
The feds never found what they were searching for.

471
00:36:54,060 --> 00:36:57,080
It was hidden in 21's secret wine cellar.

472
00:36:59,940 --> 00:37:03,700
Only by inserting a slender 18-inch length of wire

473
00:37:03,701 --> 00:37:06,560
would the secret door silently slide back

474
00:37:06,561 --> 00:37:09,460
to reveal 21's most coveted treasure,

475
00:37:12,740 --> 00:37:15,060
2,000 cases of wine.

476
00:37:16,940 --> 00:37:19,120
Today, that very same wine cellar

477
00:37:19,121 --> 00:37:22,340
still must be unlocked with that 18-inch wire.

478
00:37:26,440 --> 00:37:29,700
By 1929, the stock market crashed,

479
00:37:30,200 --> 00:37:31,520
followed by the Great Depression.

480
00:37:31,521 --> 00:37:37,620
In spite of the temperance movement's best intentions,

481
00:37:38,740 --> 00:37:41,060
alcohol-related crime increased.

482
00:37:42,980 --> 00:37:45,260
But if we have to keep the dry states

483
00:37:45,261 --> 00:37:47,940
and at the same time keep the wet states,

484
00:37:48,460 --> 00:37:50,740
prohibition will remain the football of politics

485
00:37:50,741 --> 00:37:52,960
and there will be no settlement of it.

486
00:37:53,440 --> 00:37:55,480
The American people were fed up.

487
00:37:56,120 --> 00:37:58,640
They wanted to drink, legally.

488
00:37:58,641 --> 00:38:04,700
FDR was elected on a platform that included repeal of the 18th Amendment.

489
00:38:05,980 --> 00:38:08,100
The year was 1933.

490
00:38:09,800 --> 00:38:16,100
By 1935, Anheuser-Busch would begin to ship its brew in both steel and aluminum barrels,

491
00:38:16,640 --> 00:38:22,060
as well as in a new sort of container, the stainless steel beer can.

492
00:38:24,800 --> 00:38:28,120
This was an invention created by the American Can Company.

493
00:38:28,640 --> 00:38:31,960
until metal containers were lined with a thin membrane.

494
00:38:32,880 --> 00:38:36,760
Beer could not be canned because the metal would impart a foul taste to the beer.

495
00:38:38,340 --> 00:38:41,620
Actually, it was very beneficial to the brewer for getting stainless steel

496
00:38:41,621 --> 00:38:44,140
because the barrels held up better than the wooden barrels.

497
00:38:44,600 --> 00:38:46,460
You weren't worried about cracking the wood.

498
00:38:46,580 --> 00:38:48,160
You didn't have to worry about a pitch.

499
00:38:48,280 --> 00:38:50,860
Now, the barrels were lined, what they called keg line,

500
00:38:50,861 --> 00:38:52,880
and this would keep the beer away from the metal,

501
00:38:52,960 --> 00:38:54,780
and it did not pick up that metallic taste.

502
00:38:54,781 --> 00:38:59,540
Before Prohibition became the law,

503
00:39:00,120 --> 00:39:04,140
there were at least 3,500 breweries in operation in the United States.

504
00:39:05,080 --> 00:39:09,340
By the time it was finally repealed, there were less than 200.

505
00:39:11,960 --> 00:39:13,960
The saloon fared even worse.

506
00:39:15,640 --> 00:39:20,140
Ironically, most historians agree that because of the swiftly changing technology,

507
00:39:21,040 --> 00:39:25,120
The saloon was a dying institution, even before Prohibition.

508
00:39:26,120 --> 00:39:29,680
All Prohibition did was to hasten the process.

509
00:39:31,980 --> 00:39:37,100
Next, the return of the old brewery saloon in modern San Francisco.

510
00:39:38,800 --> 00:39:44,060
In 18th century England, corkscrews were manufactured by gunsmiths.

511
00:39:44,061 --> 00:39:52,680
In the 21st century, San Francisco's Barbary Coast proves that everything old is new again.

512
00:39:55,200 --> 00:40:04,580
When the earthquake leveled San Francisco in 1906, it took with it a venerable old Barbary Coast saloon, the Albatross.

513
00:40:04,940 --> 00:40:11,160
It was rebuilt and managed to survive national prohibition as a restaurant that served clams and oysters.

514
00:40:14,060 --> 00:40:18,700
In the 1930s, it was the hub of Barbary Coast nightlife.

515
00:40:21,600 --> 00:40:24,880
This historic building was a place where legends were made.

516
00:40:26,240 --> 00:40:30,020
Back in 1913, Jack Dempsey worked here.

517
00:40:30,860 --> 00:40:34,040
By 1919, he would be the world heavyweight boxing champion.

518
00:40:37,060 --> 00:40:40,200
Today, this building, renovated in 1977,

519
00:40:40,940 --> 00:40:44,040
is the oldest standing saloon in the Barbary Coast.

520
00:40:44,060 --> 00:40:51,500
It also houses the first brew pub established in San Francisco,

521
00:40:51,840 --> 00:40:55,460
a place that, like the earliest Old West saloons,

522
00:40:56,160 --> 00:40:58,600
combines a brewery and a drinking establishment,

523
00:40:58,960 --> 00:41:00,520
all in one location.

524
00:41:01,340 --> 00:41:03,100
We make our own beer by hand,

525
00:41:03,660 --> 00:41:05,920
and it harkens back to the days

526
00:41:05,921 --> 00:41:10,500
where saloons and taverns were social places

527
00:41:10,501 --> 00:41:13,720
where people could sit and have a conversation.

528
00:41:14,060 --> 00:41:16,500
I loved it, so I'm listening to Johnny Cash singing

529
00:41:16,501 --> 00:41:17,640
and I don't know where.

530
00:41:18,780 --> 00:41:21,800
The most famous antique feature in this establishment

531
00:41:21,801 --> 00:41:25,600
is the bar, a solid plank of mahogany

532
00:41:25,601 --> 00:41:27,760
that runs the entire length of the saloon.

533
00:41:29,600 --> 00:41:32,240
The last owner believed this bar was a product

534
00:41:32,241 --> 00:41:34,500
of the famous Brunswick Company in Chicago.

535
00:41:37,020 --> 00:41:40,400
In the adjoining room is the handmade copper brew kettle.

536
00:41:40,401 --> 00:41:50,655
Most brew kettles today are made from stainless steel, but this one was crafted by hand by one of the nation's foremost coppersmiths, Fred H.

537
00:41:50,656 --> 00:41:54,800
Zaft, specifically for the San Francisco Brewing Company.

538
00:41:57,800 --> 00:42:03,200
In fact, it was designed and built in San Francisco and trucked to the brew pub in pieces.

539
00:42:03,201 --> 00:42:14,200
The large kettle just made it through the front door, with less than an inch to spare.

540
00:42:14,201 --> 00:42:21,360
Then it was assembled right in the place it stands today.

541
00:42:21,361 --> 00:42:30,580
It is here, in what is called the brew house, where the art of ancient beer making begins.

542
00:42:30,581 --> 00:42:32,096
a multi-tank operation.

543
00:42:32,097 --> 00:42:36,380
At different times during the day, barley and hops will be added.

544
00:42:37,580 --> 00:42:40,323
The brew will be strained and heated.

545
00:42:40,324 --> 00:42:44,400
Finally, the boiling liquid will flow out of the copper

546
00:42:44,401 --> 00:42:49,920
kettle through a hose and down into the fermentation and cooling tanks in the basement.

547
00:42:51,960 --> 00:42:58,620
The beer is then green beer and needs to be aged. So we transfer it to another tank,

548
00:42:58,621 --> 00:43:02,300
the final tank, which is the aging and serving tank.

549
00:43:03,020 --> 00:43:06,100
In the aging and serving tank, the beer sits for a while,

550
00:43:06,540 --> 00:43:09,640
and then we hook up a hose of the tank

551
00:43:09,641 --> 00:43:11,840
and send it right up to your glass here at the bar.

552
00:43:17,260 --> 00:43:20,320
This saloon keeper even reaches back through time

553
00:43:20,321 --> 00:43:22,200
to serve his customers' takeout

554
00:43:22,201 --> 00:43:25,300
in an updated version of the old growler.

555
00:43:25,301 --> 00:43:33,360
Like their modern counterparts, saloons in the Old West were more often places of friendship

556
00:43:33,361 --> 00:43:34,399
and community.

557
00:43:34,400 --> 00:43:40,260
The old images of incessant bar fights and shootings were probably exaggerated.

558
00:43:41,060 --> 00:43:45,740
But one thing we do know for sure, for at least a hundred years,

559
00:43:46,440 --> 00:43:48,540
they were the only place to get a drink.

